1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,240
Okay, moving on to disorders of pigmentation and melanocytes.

2
00:00:03,620 --> 00:00:07,080
Before you can understand these disorders, you've got to understand the melanocytes.

3
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:08,440
So let's briefly review.

4
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:11,520
The melanocytes are responsible for skin pigmentation.

5
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:15,160
Recall that they take tyrosine, which is a precursor molecule,

6
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:17,940
and then they use that to make melanin,

7
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,660
and they make melanin within organelles called melanosomes,

8
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:23,100
and that's important for board exams.

9
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,940
They'd like you to know that the melanin is made in the melanosomes.

10
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:30,420
Now, the melanosomes are then passed off to the keratinocytes,

11
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:32,560
and when they're handed to the keratinocytes,

12
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:34,340
that then results in skin pigmentation.

13
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:36,500
So let's go back and review briefly.

14
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:38,140
This is your epidermis.

15
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:39,220
This is your dermis.

16
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:41,180
Of course, you've got these basal cells,

17
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:44,760
and then on top of these basal cells, you've got additional keratinocytes,

18
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,500
and within the basal cells, just at the level of the basal cells,

19
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:51,840
you've got additional cells in here, which are melanocytes.

20
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:54,040
Now, the melanocytes take tyrosine.

21
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:56,900
They make melanin, again, within the melanosomes,

22
00:00:57,260 --> 00:01:00,360
and then they pass the melanosomes to the keratinocytes,

23
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:04,800
and the keratinocytes then take up that melanin and become pigmented.

24
00:01:05,420 --> 00:01:08,000
So that's the basic understanding of the melanocytes.

25
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:10,560
Again, they're responsible for skin pigmentation.

26
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:13,580
They're present in the basal layer of the epidermis,

27
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:17,660
and they synthesize melanin in melanosomes, again, from tyrosine.

28
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:19,960
Now, one other important high-yield point,

29
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:23,440
and that is that the melanocytes are derived from the neural crest,

30
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:25,980
and board examiners like you to know that as well.

31
00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:28,440
Now, the first disorder is vitiligo,

32
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:32,160
and this is when you get autoimmune destruction of the melanocytes,

33
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:36,060
which results in a localized loss of skin pigmentation.

34
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:40,520
So the idea here is that if this is the epidermal-dermal junction,

35
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:44,400
and you've got these basal cells here in the epidermis,

36
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:47,360
and, of course, the melanocytes are here, here, and here,

37
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:51,620
imagine if you knock out these melanocytes focally in a localized region,

38
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:55,400
then this area of the skin, for example, would become depigmented,

39
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:56,800
and that's called vitiligo.

40
00:01:57,380 --> 00:01:59,080
Here's a clinical image of vitiligo.

41
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:01,980
Notice that this patient has normal pigmentation here,

42
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:04,080
but has depigmentation here,

43
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:08,360
and, again, that is due to autoimmune destruction of the melanocytes,

44
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:09,060
very high-yield.

45
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:10,460
Board examiners want you to know

46
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:13,860
that there is autoimmune destruction of the melanocytes that is localized.

47
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:16,660
Now, in addition, because this is an autoimmune disease,

48
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:19,480
it can be associated with other autoimmune diseases,

49
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:21,280
but that's nothing that we have to memorize.

50
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:22,560
That's a general principle.

51
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:25,160
And then, finally, the point I'd like to make

52
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:26,840
is that in a dark-skinned individual,

53
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:29,660
you'll clearly see that the skin becomes depigmented.

54
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:31,640
However, in a lighter-skinned individual,

55
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:33,580
the only way that you might pick this up,

56
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:34,740
especially on a board exam,

57
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:36,920
is when the patient goes to tan,

58
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:38,660
or when the patient's out in the sun,

59
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:41,620
certain areas of the skin will not become dark,

60
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:44,020
and that's because they don't have melanocytes,

61
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:45,260
and that would be a sign

62
00:02:45,260 --> 00:02:47,420
that there's autoimmune destruction of the melanocytes,

63
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:50,660
so fail to tan certain areas of the skin.

64
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:53,660
Albinism is a congenital defect

65
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:55,540
where the patients lack pigmentation.

66
00:02:56,240 --> 00:02:58,100
It is due to an enzyme defect,

67
00:02:58,340 --> 00:02:59,860
usually tyrosinase,

68
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:01,760
that then impairs melanin production.

69
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:04,640
Now, again, the idea is that if you don't have tyrosinase,

70
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:06,960
you can't take tyrosine and make melanin,

71
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:10,540
and so you do need to know this for the examinations as well.

72
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:12,640
Now, it can involve only the eyes,

73
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:14,040
which is called an ocular form,

74
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:16,600
or it can involve both the eyes and the skin,

75
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:18,620
which is called the oculocutaneous form.

76
00:03:18,980 --> 00:03:20,340
And, of course, when it involves the skin,

77
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:22,560
it'll diffusely involve the entire skin.

78
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:24,940
And there's gonna be an increased risk for skin cancer.

79
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:26,620
Of course, if you don't have melanin,

80
00:03:26,660 --> 00:03:29,000
you can't protect against UVB sunlight,

81
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:30,260
so there'll be an increased risk

82
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:33,380
in formation of pyrimidine dimers in the DNA.

83
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:35,480
Now, again, what would be the three cancers

84
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:36,200
that you would get?

85
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:38,200
Number one, basal cell carcinoma.

86
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:40,180
Number two, squamous cell carcinoma.

87
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:41,680
And number three, melanoma,

88
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:43,680
which we're gonna talk about in a few minutes.

89
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:47,020
A freckle is a small tan-bron macule.

90
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:49,240
It darkens when exposed to sunlight.

91
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:51,080
And board examiners want you to know

92
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:52,720
why is a freckle a little darker

93
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:53,860
than the rest of the skin,

94
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:57,380
and that's because it has increased numbers of melanosomes,

95
00:03:57,480 --> 00:03:59,800
very high yield for the purposes of board exams.

96
00:04:00,220 --> 00:04:03,240
There is an increased number of melanosomes in a freckle.

97
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:05,380
There are no increased melanocytes,

98
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:07,800
only increased melanosomes.

99
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:11,980
Melasma is a mask-like hyperpigmentation of the cheeks

100
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:14,140
that's classically associated with pregnancy

101
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:15,820
and oral contraceptives.

102
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:18,040
The next disorder is a nevus,

103
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:21,220
and this is a neoplasm of melanocytes,

104
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:22,220
but it's benign.

105
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:24,320
And we classically call this a mole.

106
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:26,680
Now, nevus can be congenital,

107
00:04:26,940 --> 00:04:28,400
which is usually present at birth,

108
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:30,720
and one of the features of a congenital nevus

109
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:32,680
is that you often see hair growing from it.

110
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:33,660
So, for example,

111
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:35,580
here's a picture of a congenital nevus

112
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:36,680
that was present at birth,

113
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:38,680
and you can see there's these little strands

114
00:04:38,680 --> 00:04:39,840
of hair growing from it,

115
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:41,620
and so when you see hair growing from it,

116
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:43,000
that tells you that it's a nevus

117
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,980
because a melanoma would not have hair growing from it.

118
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:47,160
Just think about the fact

119
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:50,160
that melanoma is a malignant proliferation of melanocytes,

120
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:52,560
and a malignant proliferation of melanocytes

121
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:54,220
is simply going to destroy skin.

122
00:04:54,220 --> 00:04:56,900
It's not going to skip around the hair follicles

123
00:04:56,900 --> 00:04:58,820
and leave the hair follicles in place.

124
00:04:59,140 --> 00:05:00,600
It would grow through the skin

125
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:02,180
and would destroy the hair follicles

126
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:03,680
along with the rest of the skin.

127
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:05,440
And so the fact that you see here

128
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:06,460
coming from this lesion

129
00:05:06,460 --> 00:05:08,880
means that the cells are sort of respecting

130
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:09,800
the hair follicles

131
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:11,480
and growing around the hair follicles,

132
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:13,660
and that would be a sign that you have a nevus

133
00:05:13,660 --> 00:05:16,280
or a benign proliferation of melanocytes.

134
00:05:16,780 --> 00:05:18,500
Now, of course, this is a congenital nevus,

135
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:20,880
but you can also get acquired nevi,

136
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:22,860
and those arise later in life.

137
00:05:22,860 --> 00:05:23,920
And so these are the moles

138
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:26,200
that we develop over time as we age.

139
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:28,960
When you have a nevus arise later in life,

140
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:29,820
what you need to know

141
00:05:29,820 --> 00:05:32,800
is that here is the epidermal-dermal junction,

142
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:35,460
and this is where the melanocytes would be present.

143
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:38,580
And when these melanocytes grow to produce the mole,

144
00:05:38,920 --> 00:05:40,420
the first thing that they're going to do

145
00:05:40,420 --> 00:05:41,760
is they're going to grow

146
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:44,320
along the dermal-epidermal junction.

147
00:05:44,940 --> 00:05:45,860
And so when they grow

148
00:05:45,860 --> 00:05:47,600
along the dermal-epidermal junction,

149
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:52,380
that is called a junctional nevus.

150
00:05:52,620 --> 00:05:54,200
Now, a junctional nevus is actually

151
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:55,560
the most common mole in children.

152
00:05:55,880 --> 00:05:56,840
And so you can think about it,

153
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:58,220
if it's going to start out this way

154
00:05:58,220 --> 00:05:59,740
and it starts a little later in life,

155
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:01,040
then it'll happen in children,

156
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:02,400
and when it happens in children,

157
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:04,700
that would be the most common mole in children.

158
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:07,840
Eventually, these cells will then extend down

159
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:09,240
into the dermis as well.

160
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:11,860
And when they extend down into the dermis,

161
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:14,040
that's called a compound nevus.

162
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:16,540
Finally, this junctional component

163
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:17,660
will actually disappear.

164
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:19,880
And when the junctional component disappears,

165
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:21,320
the only thing that will be left

166
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:23,380
will be the dermal component,

167
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:26,880
and we call that a intradermal nevus.

168
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:28,280
Now, the intradermal nevus

169
00:06:28,280 --> 00:06:30,020
is the most common mole in adults,

170
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:32,220
and that's because that's the very last stage.

171
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:35,420
And so if it starts in a child as a junctional nevus,

172
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:38,600
it'll end up in an adult as an intradermal nevus.

173
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:41,960
And so adults classically develop intradermal nevi.

174
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:44,680
Now, you can get dysplasia within a mole,

175
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:47,220
and that could be a precursor to melanoma.

176
00:06:47,900 --> 00:06:51,460
Melanoma is a malignant neoplasm of melanocytes.

177
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:54,900
It's the most common cause of death from skin cancer.

178
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:56,800
Now, remember that your key skin cancers

179
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,220
are basal cell carcinoma,

180
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:00,640
squamous cell carcinoma, and melanoma.

181
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:02,440
And of course, basal cell carcinoma

182
00:07:02,440 --> 00:07:03,660
and squamous cell carcinoma

183
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:05,140
have an excellent prognosis,

184
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:07,100
and so your most common cause of death

185
00:07:07,100 --> 00:07:09,220
from skin cancer will be melanoma.

186
00:07:09,220 --> 00:07:11,680
The risk factors are all based on exposure

187
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:13,820
to UVB-induced DNA damage.

188
00:07:14,220 --> 00:07:15,300
So the more you see sun,

189
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:17,020
the more you're gonna see UVB light.

190
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:19,000
If you have albinism,

191
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:21,040
you won't be protected against UVB light.

192
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:23,340
And if you have 0-dermapigmentosum,

193
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:25,120
you won't have the enzymes necessary

194
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:27,820
to repair the DNA damage that occurs

195
00:07:27,820 --> 00:07:29,500
due to UVB light.

196
00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:31,140
An additional risk factor

197
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:32,620
is called dysplastic nevus syndrome.

198
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:34,820
This is an autosomal dominant disorder

199
00:07:34,820 --> 00:07:36,700
where the patients get dysplastic nevi,

200
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:39,700
and, of course, dysplasia is a precursor to cancer,

201
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:41,600
and so this can progress to melanoma.

202
00:07:42,040 --> 00:07:43,320
Now, when you have a melanoma,

203
00:07:43,620 --> 00:07:45,880
remember that it's going to be a mole-like growth,

204
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:48,980
and so we need to distinguish melanoma from mole.

205
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:50,440
And the way by which we do that

206
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:52,180
is to look at the ABCDs.

207
00:07:52,220 --> 00:07:52,960
So, for example,

208
00:07:53,420 --> 00:07:54,660
if it's asymmetric,

209
00:07:55,040 --> 00:07:56,580
then we think more of melanoma.

210
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:58,340
If the borders are irregular,

211
00:07:58,740 --> 00:08:00,200
we think more about melanoma.

212
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:02,140
If the color is not uniform,

213
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:03,920
we think more about melanoma.

214
00:08:03,920 --> 00:08:06,540
And if the diameter is greater than 6 millimeters,

215
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:09,080
then we'd also be more concerned about melanoma.

216
00:08:09,580 --> 00:08:11,260
Now, here's a classic picture of a melanoma.

217
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:13,040
Let me just highlight these principles.

218
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:16,300
Number one, you can see that this is asymmetric.

219
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:19,620
Number two, you can see that the borders are not regular.

220
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:21,120
You can see that they're jagged.

221
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:22,260
Number three,

222
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:24,420
you can see that the color is not uniform.

223
00:08:25,060 --> 00:08:26,060
Some areas are darker,

224
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:27,520
and some areas are lighter.

225
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:28,760
And number four,

226
00:08:28,860 --> 00:08:29,620
if you had a ruler,

227
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:31,040
you would see that the diameter

228
00:08:31,040 --> 00:08:32,440
is greater than 6 millimeters.

229
00:08:32,600 --> 00:08:33,180
But there, of course,

230
00:08:33,260 --> 00:08:34,280
there is no ruler here.

231
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:36,260
Now, melanoma is characterized

232
00:08:36,260 --> 00:08:37,600
by two growth phases.

233
00:08:38,040 --> 00:08:39,660
It starts with radial growth

234
00:08:39,660 --> 00:08:40,700
that goes horizontally

235
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:42,080
along the epidermis

236
00:08:42,080 --> 00:08:43,380
and the superficial dermis.

237
00:08:43,780 --> 00:08:44,520
And then eventually,

238
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:46,260
there can be a vertical growth phase

239
00:08:46,260 --> 00:08:48,720
where it begins to grow deep into the dermis.

240
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:50,320
Let's just highlight this briefly.

241
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:52,380
So here is the dermal-epidermal junction.

242
00:08:52,940 --> 00:08:54,960
Again, epidermis, dermis.

243
00:08:55,220 --> 00:08:55,940
And melanocytes,

244
00:08:56,040 --> 00:08:56,960
they normally live here.

245
00:08:57,540 --> 00:08:58,960
Now, in the very early phases,

246
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:00,640
you get something called a radial growth

247
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:02,240
where the tumor cells

248
00:09:02,240 --> 00:09:04,980
basically move radially,

249
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:07,140
horizontally in this way.

250
00:09:07,340 --> 00:09:08,540
So it can evolve, for example,

251
00:09:08,620 --> 00:09:09,480
the epidermis

252
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:12,600
and maybe the superficial parts of the dermis.

253
00:09:12,940 --> 00:09:14,200
And that's the initial phase

254
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:15,880
called the radial growth phase.

255
00:09:16,300 --> 00:09:17,900
Now, during this radial growth phase,

256
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:20,300
there is a very low risk for metastasis.

257
00:09:20,780 --> 00:09:21,600
And the reason is

258
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:22,920
that the key feature,

259
00:09:23,060 --> 00:09:23,880
very high yield,

260
00:09:24,260 --> 00:09:25,500
the key feature

261
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:28,120
that results in an increased risk

262
00:09:28,120 --> 00:09:29,160
for metastasis

263
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:30,680
is depth of invasion.

264
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:32,560
The deeper these cells go,

265
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:34,280
the more likely it is to spread

266
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:35,620
to other parts of the body

267
00:09:35,620 --> 00:09:37,460
and the worse the prognosis.

268
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:38,380
So, of course,

269
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:40,560
if you have a radial phase

270
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:41,300
in which the cells

271
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:42,280
are growing horizontally,

272
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:44,080
there wouldn't be a very high risk.

273
00:09:44,440 --> 00:09:44,780
However,

274
00:09:44,920 --> 00:09:46,260
during the vertical growth phase,

275
00:09:46,340 --> 00:09:47,760
the cells begin to grow down.

276
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:49,800
And as the cells begin to grow down,

277
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:51,700
that then increases the risk

278
00:09:51,700 --> 00:09:53,280
for metastasis

279
00:09:53,280 --> 00:09:55,920
and makes the prognosis worse.

280
00:09:56,700 --> 00:09:57,940
Melanoma can be divided

281
00:09:57,940 --> 00:09:59,980
into four major subtypes.

282
00:10:00,320 --> 00:10:02,980
The first is lentigomaligna melanoma.

283
00:10:03,420 --> 00:10:04,680
And the idea here

284
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:05,800
is that you've got

285
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:08,300
your epidermal-dermal junction.

286
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:11,740
And in lentigomaligna melanoma,

287
00:10:12,120 --> 00:10:13,280
you get a lentiginous.

288
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:14,640
Lentiginous basically means

289
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:15,420
that you remain

290
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:17,880
along the dermal-epidermal junction.

291
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:20,360
So you get a lentiginous proliferation

292
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:21,700
of melanocytes

293
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:22,700
along this junction.

294
00:10:23,240 --> 00:10:23,700
Now, of course,

295
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:24,560
if the melanocytes

296
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:25,540
are only growing

297
00:10:25,540 --> 00:10:26,420
in this line

298
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:27,380
along this junction,

299
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:28,360
the prognosis

300
00:10:28,360 --> 00:10:29,440
is going to be very good

301
00:10:29,440 --> 00:10:30,740
because you're not really

302
00:10:30,740 --> 00:10:31,580
going deep.

303
00:10:31,840 --> 00:10:33,300
You're only going horizontally,

304
00:10:33,540 --> 00:10:34,340
i.e. there's only

305
00:10:34,340 --> 00:10:35,740
a radial growth phase.

306
00:10:36,180 --> 00:10:36,780
And so that's why

307
00:10:36,780 --> 00:10:37,760
I say here in the text

308
00:10:37,760 --> 00:10:39,520
that it has a good prognosis

309
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:40,940
because of radial growth.

310
00:10:41,740 --> 00:10:43,140
Superficial spreading melanoma

311
00:10:43,140 --> 00:10:44,480
is the most common subtype.

312
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:45,940
You get a dominant

313
00:10:45,940 --> 00:10:47,380
early radial phase.

314
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:48,460
And because of that,

315
00:10:48,540 --> 00:10:49,580
you get a good prognosis.

316
00:10:49,780 --> 00:10:50,060
Again,

317
00:10:50,620 --> 00:10:52,040
if this is the

318
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:53,520
epidermal-dermal junction

319
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:54,540
and you've got

320
00:10:54,540 --> 00:10:55,180
your melanocytes

321
00:10:55,180 --> 00:10:55,780
growing here

322
00:10:55,780 --> 00:10:57,200
and they're superficial spreading,

323
00:10:57,340 --> 00:10:59,160
they stay in the superficial dermis

324
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:00,500
and in the epidermis,

325
00:11:00,780 --> 00:11:01,740
then that's predominantly

326
00:11:01,740 --> 00:11:02,940
a radial growth phase.

327
00:11:03,280 --> 00:11:03,820
And so therefore,

328
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:05,220
because they haven't gone down,

329
00:11:05,580 --> 00:11:06,200
it's going to have

330
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:07,440
a good prognosis.

331
00:11:07,580 --> 00:11:08,600
And that's what I say here.

332
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:09,760
It's a dominant

333
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:11,400
early radial phase

334
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:12,680
with good prognosis.

335
00:11:12,760 --> 00:11:13,440
And that's what we mean

336
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:15,180
by superficial spreading.

337
00:11:15,180 --> 00:11:16,540
It doesn't go deep.

338
00:11:17,260 --> 00:11:17,940
The third type

339
00:11:17,940 --> 00:11:19,960
is called a nodular melanoma.

340
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:20,840
And this has

341
00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:22,340
an early vertical phase.

342
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:23,620
And the idea here

343
00:11:23,620 --> 00:11:25,300
is that if this is

344
00:11:25,300 --> 00:11:27,520
the epidermal-dermal junction,

345
00:11:28,040 --> 00:11:28,960
then the tumor cells

346
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:29,980
begin to grow

347
00:11:29,980 --> 00:11:30,700
and they start

348
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:31,940
growing down very early.

349
00:11:32,320 --> 00:11:33,480
Now when they grow down,

350
00:11:33,580 --> 00:11:34,100
what they do

351
00:11:34,100 --> 00:11:34,680
is they push

352
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:36,100
the epidermis up.

353
00:11:36,340 --> 00:11:36,980
And as they push

354
00:11:36,980 --> 00:11:37,900
the epidermis up,

355
00:11:37,960 --> 00:11:38,660
that's going to create

356
00:11:38,660 --> 00:11:40,440
a bump on the skin,

357
00:11:40,740 --> 00:11:41,660
which is why it's called

358
00:11:41,660 --> 00:11:42,920
nodular melanoma,

359
00:11:42,920 --> 00:11:43,640
because you get

360
00:11:43,640 --> 00:11:45,000
this nodule on the skin.

361
00:11:45,480 --> 00:11:47,280
It has an early vertical phase

362
00:11:47,280 --> 00:11:49,760
and a poor prognosis.

363
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:51,560
The last type of melanoma

364
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:51,960
is called

365
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:53,580
acrolentiginous melanoma.

366
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:54,580
This arises

367
00:11:54,580 --> 00:11:56,100
on the palms and soles.

368
00:11:56,420 --> 00:11:57,220
That's high yield.

369
00:11:57,300 --> 00:11:57,700
And that's what

370
00:11:57,700 --> 00:11:58,740
the word acrol means.

371
00:11:59,160 --> 00:12:00,560
And it often occurs

372
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:01,800
in dark-skinned individuals.

373
00:12:01,960 --> 00:12:03,180
That's also very high yield.

374
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:04,200
And it so happens

375
00:12:04,200 --> 00:12:04,840
that this type

376
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:05,700
is not related

377
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:07,120
to UV light exposure.

378
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:08,560
And that concludes

379
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:09,140
our section

380
00:12:09,140 --> 00:12:09,880
on disorders

381
00:12:09,880 --> 00:12:10,760
of pigmentation

382
00:12:10,760 --> 00:12:11,960
and melanocytes.

